MLOPS Interview Series – Part 2

1️⃣ What are the stages of the ML lifecycle?
Answer: Data Collection → Data Preparation → Model Training → Model Evaluation → Model Deployment → Monitoring & Maintenance

2️⃣ What is the difference between model training and model deployment?
Answer:
Training: Building the model using historical data. Focus is on accuracy. 
Deployment: Making the model available for predictions in production. Focus is on speed and reliability. 

3️⃣ How do you track experiments in MLOps?
Answer:
Track every training run by logging:
Hyperparameters (learning rate, batch size)
Metrics (accuracy, loss)
Model files
Dataset used
Code version
Tools: MLflow, Weights & Biases, Neptune.ai

4️⃣ What is a model registry?
Answer:
A central place to store and manage all trained models.
Features:
Track model versions (v1.0, v1.1, v2.0)
Store metadata (accuracy, training date)
Mark stages (Development, Staging, Production)
Enable easy rollback

Example: MLflow Model Registry, SageMaker Model Registry

5️⃣ How do you detect data drift?
Answer:
Data drift = when production data looks different from training data.
Detection methods:
Compare feature statistics (mean, min, max)
Statistical tests (KS test, PSI)
Monitor distributions over time

Tools: EvidentlyAI, SageMaker Model Monitor, Azure Drift Monitor

6️⃣ How do you monitor models in production?
Answer:
Monitor four things:
Model metrics: Accuracy, predictions quality
Data quality: Missing values, outliers, drift
System metrics: API latency, error rates, CPU/memory
Business metrics: User engagement, revenue impact
Tools: Prometheus, Grafana, CloudWatch, Azure Monitor

7️⃣ What is model explainability?
Answer:
Understanding WHY a model made a specific prediction.
Techniques:
SHAP: Shows which features contributed most
LIME: Explains individual predictions
Feature importance: Ranks features by impact

8️⃣ How does automated model retraining work?
Answer:
Automated retraining updates models without manual work.
Triggers:
Scheduled (weekly/monthly)
Performance drop (accuracy below threshold)
Data drift detected
New data available
Process: Detect trigger → Fetch new data → Train model → Evaluate → Deploy if better
Benefit: Keeps models accurate without manual intervention.

9️⃣ How does MLOps support reproducibility?
Answer:
Reproducibility = getting same results when rerunning experiments.
How to achieve:
Version code (Git)
Version data (DVC, LakeFS)
Version models (Model registry)
Use Docker for consistent environments
Set random seeds
Log everything in experiment tracking

